<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aeolians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aeolian Greeks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aeolians were one of the main divisions of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, speaking the Aeolic dialect, at home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>. Around 1000 BCE Aeolian settlers crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and the north-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, founding cities such as Mytilene, Cyme and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-smyrna">Old Smyrna</a>. Their region, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolis">Aeolis</a>, produced the lyric poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sappho">Sappho</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcaeus">Alcaeus</a>.</p></div>
